{
  "id": "c4",
  "title": "if and else",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c4-l1",
      "title": "Making Decisions",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "if (condition) runs the next statement only when the condition is true. else gives the other path. Put several statements in braces { } to run them together.",
        "Comparisons: == equal, != not equal, < > <= >=. In C, true is any number except 0, and a comparison gives 1 or 0.",
        "Careful: = stores, == compares. if (x = 5) stores 5 in x, which counts as true, so the if always runs. It's one of the most famous bugs in C."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int temp = 31;",
          "    if (temp > 25) {",
          "        printf(\"hot\\n\");",
          "    } else {",
          "        printf(\"not hot\\n\");",
          "    }",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "hot"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c4-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Which operator checks if two values are equal?",
          "options": [
            "==",
            "=",
            "!=",
            "==="
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "== compares, = stores a value."
        },
        {
