    {
      "id": "c4-l3",
      "title": "switch",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "When one value can be many different things, switch is tidier than a long else-if chain. switch (x) jumps to the matching case.",
        "Each case usually ends with break;. Without it, C keeps running into the next case. That's called fall-through, sometimes useful, often a bug.",
        "default: catches every value that has no case of its own."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int day = 3;",
          "    switch (day) {",
          "    case 1:",
          "        printf(\"Monday\\n\");",
          "        break;",
          "    case 2:",
          "        printf(\"Tuesday\\n\");",
          "        break;",
          "    case 3:",
          "        printf(\"Wednesday\\n\");",
          "        break;",
          "    default:",
          "        printf(\"later\\n\");",
          "    }",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "Wednesday"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c4-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does break do inside a switch?",
          "options": [
            "Leaves the switch",
            "Stops the program",
            "Jumps to default",
            "Nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Without break, the next case would run as well."
        },
        {
          "id": "c4-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    int n = 2;",
            "    switch (n) {",
            "    case 1:",
            "        printf(\"one\\n\");",
            "        break;",
            "    case 2:",
            "        printf(\"two\\n\");",
            "    case 3:",
            "        printf(\"three\\n\");",
            "        break;",
            "    }",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "options": [
            "two\nthree",
            "two",
            "three",
            "nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "case 2 has no break, so it falls through into case 3."
        },
        {
          "id": "c4-l3-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
